<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-1-integration-by-parts" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Integration By Parts</title>

<subsection xml:id="ssec-reversing-the-product-rule">
  <title> Reversing the Product Rule</title>

  <p>
  One of the most useful integration techniques from <xref ref="ssec-fundamental-integrals"> MTH 132 </xref> was the substitution method,
  which came from reversing the Chain Rule. But there is another important differentiation rule, the Product Rule, which we can also reverse to find integrals.
  Since the product rule states: <md> (f(x)\,g(x))' \ =\ f(x)\, g'(x) + g(x)\, f'(x)\,,</md>
  taking the antiderivative of both sides gives:
<md> f(x)\,g(x) \ =\ \int\! f(x)\,g'(x)\,dx + \int\! g(x)\,f'(x)\,dx\,,</md> or:
<md> \int\! f(x)\,g'(x)\,dx \ =\ f(x)\,g(x) - \int\! g(x)\,f'(x)\,dx\,.</md>
In Leibnitz notation, taking <m>u=f(x)</m>, <m>du=f'(x)\,dx</m>
and <m>v=g(x)</m>, <m>dv = g'(x)\,dx</m>:
<md> \int\! u\ dv \ =\ uv \,-\, \int\! v\ du\,. </md>
This method transforms the integral of a product <m>f(x)\,g'(x)</m>
into <m>f(x) \,g(x)</m> minus the integral of <m>g(x)\,f'(x)</m>, the other term in the Product Rule; we can 
think of lowering <m>f(x)</m> to its derivative <m>f'(x)</m> and raising <m>g'(x)</m> to its antiderivative <m>g(x)</m>.
  </p>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ssec-method-for-ibp">
  <title>Method for Integration by Parts</title>
  <p>
    <ol>
      <li>
       <p>Given an indefinite integral <m>\int\! h(x)\,dx</m>, find a factor of the integrand <m>h(x)</m> which you recognize as the 
        derivative of a function <m>g(x)</m>: that is, write <m>h(x)=f(x)\cdot g'(x)</m>.</p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>Taking <m>u=f(x)</m>, <m>dv = g'(x)\,dx</m>, transform the integral <m>\int\! u\,dv=\int\! f(x)\, g'(x)\,dx</m> into 
          <m>uv-\int\! v\,du \ =\ f(x)\,g(x)-\int\! g(x)\,f'(x)\,dx</m></p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Simplify <m>g(x)\,f'(x)</m>, possibly using identities, and try to find its integral by other methods such as Substitution.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Sometimes you can repeat Steps 1 and 2 on <m>\int\! g(x)\,f'(x)\,dx</m>  with a <em>different</em> <m>u,v</m> 
          <fn> Repeating with the <em> same </em> factorization <m>\int\! v\,du</m> would get back the original integral <m>\int\! u\,dv</m>. </fn>. 
          This might further simplify integral so you can evaluate by other methods.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Instead of simplifying the integral, Step 3 or 4 might give an expression with the <em>same</em> 
          integral you started with. Solve the resulting equation to find that integral.
        </p>
      </li>

    </ol>
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-ibp" title="Integration by Parts Example">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate <md>\int\! x\,\cos(x)\,dx.</md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        There are two obvious candidates for <m>u,v</m>. First, if we take <m>u=\cos(x)</m>, <m>dv=x\,dx</m>, 
        we get <m>du=-\sin(x)\,dx</m>, <m>v = \frac12 x^2</m>, and:
        <sbsgroup widths = "25% 5% 20% 5% 30%" align="center">
          <sidebyside>
            <p>
              <m> \int u\,dv </m>
            </p>

            <p>
              <m> = </m>
            </p>

            <p>
              <m> uv </m>
            </p>

            <p>
              <m> - </m>
            </p>

            <p>
              <m> \int v\,du </m>
            </p>
          </sidebyside>
          <sidebyside>
            <p>
              <m> \int \cos(x)\,x\,dx </m>
            </p>

            <p>
              <m> = </m>
            </p>

            <p>
              <m> \cos(x)\left(\tfrac 12 x^2\right) </m>
            </p>

            <p>
              <m> - </m>
            </p>

            <p>
              <m> \int \tfrac12 x^2\,(-\sin(x))\,dx </m>
            </p>
          </sidebyside>
        </sbsgroup>
      </p>

      <p>
        Unfortunately, the new integral <m>\int\! x^2\sin(x)\,dx</m> is harder than the original <m>\int\! x\cos(x)\,dx</m>. 
        We must make a wiser choice of <m>u,v</m>, so that the derivative <m>du</m> will be simpler than the original <m>u</m>, 
        while the antiderivative <m>v</m> will be no worse than the original <m>dv</m>.
      </p>

      <p>The other obvious choice will work: take <m>u=x</m>, <m>dv = \cos(x)\,dx</m>, so that <m>du = 1\, dx</m> 
        and <m>v=\sin(x)</m>. Then <fn>For brevity, we neglect the constant <m>+C</m>, though you should write it in formal work.</fn>:
      </p>

      <sbsgroup widths="25% 5% 20% 5% 30%" align="center">
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int u\,dv </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m>=</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m>uv</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m>-</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m>\int v\,du</m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m>\int x\,\cos(x)\,dx</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m>x\sin(x)</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m>-</m>
          </p>
          
          <p>
            <m>\int \sin(x)\,1\,dx</m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>
          <p>
            <m>x\sin(x) </m>
          </p>
          <p>
            <m> + </m>
          </p>
          <p>
            <m> \cos(x) </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </sbsgroup>

      <p>
        To check our answer, we reverse our Integration by Parts using the Product Rule:
        <md> (x\sin(x) + \cos(x))' \ =\ x\sin'(x) + \sin(x)(x)' + \cos'(x) </md>
        <md> =\, x\cos(x) + \sin(x) - \sin(x) \ =\ x\cos(x).</md>
      </p>
    </solution>

  </example>
  
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate  <md>\int x^2\,e^{-x}\,dx.</md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We should choose <m>u=x^2</m>, <m>dv=e^{-x}</m>, so that <m>du=2x\,dx</m> is simpler, but <m>v = -e^{-x}</m> is no 
        more complicated:
      </p>

      <sbsgroup widths="25% 5% 20% 5% 30%" align="center">
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int u\,dv </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> uv </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> - </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> \int v\,du </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int x^2\,e^{-x}\,dx </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> x^2\,(-e^{-x}) </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> - </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> \int (-e^{-x})\,2x\,dx </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> -x^2\,e^{-x} </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> + </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> 2\int x\,e^{-x}\,dx </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </sbsgroup>

      <p>
        Going on to Step 4, we repeat the process for the integral on the right side, 
        this time with <m>u = x</m>, <m>dv= e^{-x}\,dx</m> and <m>du = dx</m>, <m>v = -e^{-x}</m>:
      </p>

      <sbsgroup widths="20% 5% 20% 5% 30%" align="center">
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int u\,dv </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> uv </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> - </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> \int v\,du </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int x\,e^{-x}\,dx </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> x\,(-e^{-x}) </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> - </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> \int (-e^{-x})\,dx </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> -x\,e^{-x} </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> + </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> \int e^{-x}\,dx </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> -x\,e^{-x} </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> + </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> (-e^{-x}) </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </sbsgroup>

      <p>
        Putting these together:
      </p>

      <sbsgroup widths="20% 5% 50%" align="center">
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int x^2\,e^{-x}\,dx </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> -x^2\,e^{-x} + 2(-x\,e^{-x} - e^{-x}) </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> -(x^2 + 2x + 2)e^{-x} </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </sbsgroup>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate <md>\int e^x\sin(x)\,dx.</md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Steps 1--4 give:
      </p>

      <sbsgroup widths="21% 2% 12% 2% 30% 18%" align="center">
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int e^x\sin(x)\,dx </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> e^x\sin(x) </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> - </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> \int e^x\cos(x)\,dx </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> u=\sin(x), \, v=e^x </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> e^x\sin(x) </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> - </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> \left(e^x\cos(x) + \int e^x\sin(x)\,dx\right) </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> u=\cos(x), \, v=e^x </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </sbsgroup>

      <p>
        We conclude:
      </p>

      <md>
        \int e^x\sin(x)\,dx \ = e^x\sin(x) -e^x\cos(x) -\int e^x\sin(x)\,dx.
      </md>

      <p>
        Since our integral <m>\int e^x\sin(x)\,dx</m> appears on both sides, we go to Step 5 and solve for it:
      </p>

      <md>
        \int e^x\sin(x)\,dx\ = \tfrac12\left(e^x\sin(x)-e^x\cos(x)\right).
      </md>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate <md>\int \ln(x)\,dx.</md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
       Here there does not seem to be any <m>dv</m> factor, but we can 
       always take <m>dv=1\,dx</m>, so <m>v = x</m>: 
      </p>
      <sbsgroup widths="25% 5% 20% 5% 30%" align="center">
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int u\,dv </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> uv </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> - </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> \int v\,du </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int \ln(x)\,1 dx </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> x\ln(x) </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> - </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> \int x\,\frac{1}{x}\,dx </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> x\ln(x) </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> - </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> x </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </sbsgroup>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate <fn> Notation: <m>\sin^{-1}(x)=\arcsin(x)</m>, but <m>\sin(x)^{-1}=\frac{1}{\sin(x)}=\csc(x)</m></fn> <md>\int \sin^{-1}(x)\,dx.</md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Again we must use <m>u=\sin^{-1}(x)</m> and <m>dv = 1\,dx</m>, counting 
        on the fact that <m>du</m> is simpler than <m>u</m>:
      </p>

      <sbsgroup widths="25% 5% 20% 5% 30%" align="center">
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int u\,dv </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> uv </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> - </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> \int v\,du </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int \sin^{-1}(x)\,1\,dx </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> x\sin^{-1}(x) </m>
          </p>
          <p>
            <m> - </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> \int x\,\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\,dx </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </sbsgroup>

      <p>
        Continuing Step 3, we use the substitution <m>z = 1-x^2</m> on the right-hand integral:
      </p>
      
      <sbsgroup widths="30% 5% 40%">
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int x\,\frac1{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\,dx </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> -\frac{1}{2} \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\,(-2x)\,dx\ </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> -\frac{1}{2} \int \frac{1}{\sqrt z}\, dz = -\sqrt{z} </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> -\sqrt{1-x^2} </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </sbsgroup>

      <p>
        Combining:
      </p>


      <sbsgroup widths="25% 5% 40%" align="center">
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <m> \int \sin^{-1}(x)\,dx </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> x\sin^{-1}(x) - (-\sqrt{1{-}x^2}) </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> = </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m> x\sin^{-1}(x) + \sqrt{1{-}x^2} </m>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </sbsgroup>

    </solution>
  </example>

</subsection>

</section>